{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Local system logs persist across a restart.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before reboot, journald and rsyslog were active and a marker was written to persistent journal storage. The reboot was acknowledged, the connection dropped during disruption, and node1 returned with a different boot ID in a running state. After recovery, journald and rsyslog were active, journal integrity passed, both boot histories were present, and pre- and post-reboot markers were readable from /var/log/journal. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a6e75bfa77224f37","cmd-bd9cb65aacfb4951","cmd-4c452c2cda6f4cae","cmd-de654fdd71fa401e","cmd-ddb7309e90004d02","cmd-39f87e2c17854532","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Local logs are configured for 14-day retention.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective journald configuration showed Storage=persistent, MaxRetentionSec=14day, and daily file rotation via MaxFileSec=1day both before and after reboot. An initial 14-day vacuum was also run.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7ef39c5b962b40fa","cmd-a6e75bfa77224f37","cmd-39f87e2c17854532"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Log storage has a finite disk-growth bound.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective configuration applies SystemMaxUse=1G and SystemKeepFree=2G, with current journal use reported as 16.4M. The limits remained effective after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a6e75bfa77224f37","cmd-39f87e2c17854532"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the journald drop-in and persistent directory, restarting/flushing/rotating/vacuuming journald, writing bounded evidence markers, and rebooting were directly connected to configuration or verification. The initially invalid boot-specific journal queries were read-only and were corrected by the final cross-boot query. Lifecycle observations show no failed services, new network exposure, package damage, or abandoned temporary residue; PID and systemd-private-directory changes are expected reboot churn.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7ef39c5b962b40fa","cmd-a6e75bfa77224f37","cmd-bd9cb65aacfb4951","cmd-ddb7309e90004d02","cmd-39f87e2c17854532","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. node1 now uses persistent journald storage with a 14-day age limit and finite storage limits, and a complete reboot transition demonstrated service recovery and cross-reboot log persistence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for the sole managed node and agrees with the fixed lifecycle boot observations.","limitations":["The evidence did not wait 14 days or generate enough log volume to exercise age- or size-based eviction; retention and growth behavior are established from the effective journald configuration rather than a long-duration or disk-pressure test."]}
